P5. What a strategy defined by imitation imitates
=================================================

PART A. The two readings of 'behave like a native primitive'
-----------------------------------------------------------

Exhaustive over every ordered pair in the declared domain, per width.
A width is 'native' when Rust has a primitive of exactly that size.

   W    native?     add pairs    add differ     mul pairs    mul differ
   1         no             4             1             4             0
   2         no            16             6            16             4
   3         no            64            28            64            33
   4         no           256           120           256           180
   5         no          1024           496          1024           783
   6         no          4096          2016          4096          2860
   7         no         16384          8128         16384          7976
   8        yes         65536             0         65536             0
   9         no        262144        130816        262144        256660
  10         no       1048576        523776       1048576       1025375
  11         no       4194304       2096128       4194304       4050046
  12         no       4194304       2096128       4194304       3925903
      (stepped by 2; the sweep is uniform, not a sample of a region)
  13         no       4194304       2096128       4194304       3667276
      (stepped by 4; the sweep is uniform, not a sample of a region)
  14         no       4194304       2096128       4194304       3141632
      (stepped by 8; the sweep is uniform, not a sample of a region)
  15         no       4194304       2096128       4194304       2088960
      (stepped by 16; the sweep is uniform, not a sample of a region)
  16        yes       4194304             0       4194304             0
      (stepped by 32; the sweep is uniform, not a sample of a region)

  widths where Rust HAS a primitive: the two readings differ on 0 of the
  pairs swept, which is zero, because at a native width the declared
  width IS the container width and there is nothing to disagree about.

  widths where Rust has NO primitive: 14 of 14 show the readings
  disagreeing. Those are exactly the widths arvo exists to provide.

  So 'behave like a native primitive' is a total specification on the
  widths Rust already serves and an ambiguous one on the widths it
  does not. The imitation is well defined precisely where it is not
  needed. Op's own refinement resolves which way it goes: the intent
  is the intuitive best choice, and imitation is the vehicle rather
  than the requirement.

PART B. The half of Rust's behaviour that cannot be imitated at all
------------------------------------------------------------------

Rust's `+` on a primitive has two behaviours, not one:
  release: wrap at the type's width
  debug  : panic on overflow

The panic is a runtime check. I15 forbids runtime checks without
qualification. So a strategy defined by imitating Rust can imitate
the release half and can never imitate the debug half.

What is available instead, and its exact region:

  const operands  : a compile-time refusal. `add_or_refuse(4000, 91)`
                    is a const item in this file and evaluated to 4091.
                    `add_or_refuse(8000, 8000)` in the same position
                    does not compile; see p5_mutant_const_overflow.rs
                    and its committed output.
  runtime operands: nothing. Not a weaker check, not a debug-only
                    check. Nothing, by I15.

That is a predicate rather than a defect: the debug half of the
imitation holds where both operands are const-available, and does not
hold anywhere else. Op's addendum to I13 is what makes the region
worth naming: the admissible category is whatever is available at
const time, which is wider than the typestate.

Demonstrating that the two readings are live at runtime too, at W=13:
  a=4000  b=91     rule(mod 2^13)=4091   container(mod 2^16)=4091    agree=true
  a=8000  b=8000   rule(mod 2^13)=7808   container(mod 2^16)=16000   agree=false
  a=5000  b=4000   rule(mod 2^13)=808    container(mod 2^16)=9000    agree=false
